<FONT FACE="Times"><P>This course analyzes the phenomenon of moral sentiments&#150;the fact that ordinary people have and express moral thoughts and emotions about others&#146; action and sometimes their own actions. We will  explore which sentiments there are and under what conditions they occur, and we will try to get clearer about the social functions and consequences of moral sentiments.   </P>
<P>&#9;This course is designed as a seminar and focuses on original literature (primarily from psychology and to a small extent from philosophy), so you are expected to engage in the readings and participate in the discussion of these readings.  Each week there will be an <I>introductory lecture</I> (on Thursdays) that helps you get acquainted with the topics and the approaches that are featured in the readings.  But the primary benefit for you will come from the work you put into studying the 2-4 assigned papers and the discussion that takes place on the Tuesday of the following week.  To this <I>seminar session </I>you will bring one page of reading notes (your questions, comments, ideas for studies, criticism, real-world observations, etc.) and we will discuss the assigned papers, partially guided by your reading notes.  (You should feel free to form reading groups if you are so inclined.  I can help with initiating them.)  </P>
<P>&#9;This course was planned for psychology majors with a background in social psychology.  However, students from other departments with an interest in moral sentiments are welcome, with two points of cautionary advice:  (1) Some (but not many) of the articles presuppose a fair amount of knowledge in social science methodology; and (2) the course does not analyze the problem of morality per se&#150;that is, we are not asking &quot;What is good&quot; and &quot;Do ethical principles exist?&quot; and the like.  Also, this course may not solve any of your moral struggles, but it may increase your awareness of your own and other people&#146;s moral thoughts and emotions.</P>

<B><P>Course Components and Grading </P>
</B><P>Attendance, weekly reading notes (due 1 p.m. each Tuesday), and participation during class make up 50% of your grade; a project makes up the other 50%.  This project can be a theoretical paper, a review paper (of articles we didn&#146;t read), a proposal for an empirical study, the write-up of a small-scale empirical study, a short story, a magazine essay &#133; (or you can propose yet another format to me).  You will present your project, either as a short talk or as a poster in the last week of the term. </P>
